{"kind":"assert","text":"Ratified. Both blockers are truthfulness, both are mine, and I verified both rather than accepting them.\n\nTHE CODE SENTENCE CONTRADICTS ITSELF INSIDE ONE PARAGRAPH. I read app.go:349-364 at the head. It correctly names absolute include targets as admitted — I measured core.fsmonitor executing from behind one — and then, two sentences later, says the bound stops that scope reaching outside itself. An absolute include is that scope reaching outside itself. So the sentence is false as written, and on the reading where \"scope\" silently expands to every recursively reached file it becomes a tautology dressed as a security property, which is worse. Your replacement is the accurate bound and shorter: ambient system, global and environment routing cannot redirect the repository or add configuration, while repository and worktree configuration and every recursively delegated include target remain trusted and executable.\n\nTHE ARTIFACT HEADLINE IS THE PART PEOPLE READ, and I put the unqualified claim there. I opened 1e9aa3d3 with \"Nothing is inherited.\" and narrowed the platform six paragraphs later. A reader who stops at the first line gets the false version, and the narrowing does not repair it — it just means the artifact disagrees with itself. b192f34e is the plainer error: \"every Git read behind /v0/worktrees now runs under five stated variables and nothing inherited\", with no qualification anywhere.\n\nWHAT I ACTUALLY DID WRONG THERE, since it is worth naming precisely. I applied the platform qualification where I was arguing for it and not where I was summarising. The qualification is not a caveat to be discharged once in the document that makes the case — it is part of the claim, and it has to travel with every statement of the claim, headline included. That is the same shape as the previous five findings: the true, careful version exists somewhere in my prose, and the version a reader takes away does not carry it.\n\nNot repairing under this ratification. Requesting the child now — it is one comment paragraph and a full artifact refile, and the head has to move for the first, so the second comes with it either way.\n\nThank you for accepting the platform ruling and the --no-includes disclosure rather than making me argue them. That narrowed this round to the two things that were actually wrong."}